From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mikhail Malyshev <mike.malyshev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 23:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ui/cursor: make the QEMUCursor refcount atomic

QEMUCursor.refcount is a plain int incremented and decremented with a
non-atomic read-modify-write, but it is not confined to one thread.
dbus_cursor_define() hands cursor_unref to GLib as the GDestroyNotify of
the message body it builds:

    v_data = g_variant_new_from_data(G_VARIANT_TYPE("ay"), c->data,
                                     c->width * c->height * 4, TRUE,
                                     (GDestroyNotify)cursor_unref,
                                     cursor_ref(c));

cursor_ref() runs there on the main thread, but the notify fires when the
GDBusMessage is finalised, which GLib does on the gdbus worker thread. So
two threads race on `c->refcount++` and `c->refcount--`, and a lost update
drives the count below the number of live owners.

What that costs, from a core dump taken on an edge device: a QEMUCursor
freed while both VirtIOGPU scanout[0].current_cursor and
QemuGraphicConsole->cursor still pointed at it. dpy_cursor_define() then
did

    cursor_unref(con->cursor);        /* 1 -> 0, g_free */
    con->cursor = cursor_ref(cursor); /* writes refcount into freed memory */

and the next guest cursor update freed the same block again, faulting
inside the allocator. The object in the core still read refcount == 0 with
two owners pointing at it, and the freed slot carried the allocator's
own free poison - a textbook double free.

The window is normally narrow. It widens with the number of display
listeners on a console, because every listener adds a ref/unref pair per
cursor update; the machine this was found on had accumulated seven
listeners on one console and hit it within seconds of a burst of cursor
traffic.

Make the two accessors atomic. This is the cheap fix: the alternative,
copying the cursor pixels so no reference crosses the thread boundary,
costs a 16 KiB copy on every cursor update per listener, for a hazard that
one instruction pair can remove.

Signed-off-by: Mikhail Malyshev <mike.malyshev@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/tools/qemu-xen/ui/cursor.c b/tools/qemu-xen/ui/cursor.c
index dd3853320d..0df0a54c24 100644
--- a/tools/qemu-xen/ui/cursor.c
+++ b/tools/qemu-xen/ui/cursor.c
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 #include "qemu/osdep.h"
+#include "qemu/atomic.h"
 #include "ui/console.h"
 
 #include "cursor_hidden.xpm"
@@ -109,18 +110,18 @@ QEMUCursor *cursor_alloc(uint16_t width, uint16_t height)
 
 QEMUCursor *cursor_ref(QEMUCursor *c)
 {
-    c->refcount++;
+    qatomic_inc(&c->refcount);
     return c;
 }
 
 void cursor_unref(QEMUCursor *c)
 {
-    if (c == NULL)
+    if (c == NULL) {
         return;
-    c->refcount--;
-    if (c->refcount)
-        return;
-    g_free(c);
+    }
+    if (qatomic_fetch_dec(&c->refcount) == 1) {
+        g_free(c);
+    }
 }
 
 int cursor_get_mono_bpl(QEMUCursor *c)
